window.SEARCH_DATA = {"updated": "November 18, 2026", "docs": [{"id": "00_INDEX", "file": "text/00_INDEX.md", "title": "125 Worth Avenue: Project Base, Master Index", "md": "# 125 Worth Avenue: Project Base, Master Index\n\nThe searchable knowledge base for the 125 Worth Avenue renovation in Palm Beach, FL. Start here.\n\n## Project at a glance\n- **Address:** 125 Worth Avenue, Palm Beach, FL (parcel 0380), on the north side of Worth Ave between 151 Worth Ave (west) and 101 Worth Ave / Kirkland House (east). Across the street: 150 Worth Ave (the Esplanade).\n- **Owner:** L3 Capital (contact: Whitney Robinette).\n- **Architect:** Fairfax & Sammons Architects (New York and Palm Beach).\n- **Lighting consultant:** CX Connect (Raquel Swearingen).\n- **Status (Nov 2026):** 50% Design Development. ARCOM / Town Council presentation dated **December 9, 2026**.\n- **Scope:** piers replacing columns on Worth Ave; loggia widened from about 4' to about 10'; new storefronts; ground-floor retail infill under the 2nd-floor cantilever; 6 retail suites; window and door replacement on floors 2 and 3; penthouse pergola and mechanical screening; northwest green space.\n- **Building:** 3 stories, 68,672 SF proposed (59,504 SF existing), 47.75' overall height, F.F.E. +12.64'.\n\n## People\n| Name | Role | Contact |\n|---|---|---|\n| Michael Cabrera, Assoc. AIA | Associate, Fairfax & Sammons; runs the project day to day | mcabrera@fairfaxandsammons.com, 212-255-0704 x212 |\n| Richard Sammons | Principal, Fairfax & Sammons; developing the loggia beam detail; style direction | rsammons@fairfaxandsammons.com |\n| Anne Fairfax | Principal, Fairfax & Sammons | |\n| Murat Saryyev | Fairfax & Sammons | msaryyev@fairfaxandsammons.com |\n| Johnathan | Fairfax & Sammons; adding the lantern to the north interior loggia elevation | |\n| William | Fairfax & Sammons | |\n| Svetlana Wunnenberg | Visuals and marketing; renderings | |\n| Raquel Swearingen | Lighting consultant, CX Connect | Raquel.Swearingen@cxconnect.com, O 847-499-8352 |\n| Whitney Robinette | Ownership, L3 Capital | whitney.robinette@l3capital.com |\n| Danele Spoden | RLE Partners | dspoden@rlepartners.com |\n\nFairfax & Sammons offices: 2 Sniffen Court, New York, NY 10016, and 189 Bradley Place, Palm Beach, FL 33480 (new Palm Beach address).\n\n## Timeline\n| Date | Event | Document |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026-11-17 | 50% DD presentation and scope narrative shared | `2026-11-17_arcom-presentation.md` |\n| 2026-11-17 | Round 1 lighting (Hinkley Palma, Primo BB-19E); materials review with Ownership at 2:30 PM | slide 25; `2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2.md` |\n| 2026-11-17 | Richard's feedback: more ornate, true Mizner style; recessed step lights | `2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2.md` |\n| 2026-11-18 | Round 2 lanterns forwarded; pick one for the north interior loggia elevation; beam detail section needed for rendering | `2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2.md` |\n| 2026-11-20 | Friday target for refinements (Raquel) | |\n| 2026-12-09 | ARCOM / Town Council presentation date | |\n\n## Key decisions and constraints\n- **Light spill:** no more than 0.5 footcandles over the property line. The lanterns use 3W lamps; the loggia sits at about 2 fc (5 to 10 is typical). Use the same size lantern throughout; avoid 4-lamp fixtures.\n- **Lantern style:** Richard wants ornate, authentic Mizner-style lanterns (Remains Lighting, Bonnet House references). Round 1 was rejected as too simplified.\n- **Step lights:** recessed or flush in the new piers.\n- **Materials:** coquina or concrete stone loggia floor (slab or 3 to 4 ft chevron); Haddonstone cast stone treads; Regts Delft field tile; wood-beam or stucco loggia ceiling; Farrow & Ball Wimborne White No. 239 walls.\n\n## Documents in the base\n| File | What it covers |\n|---|---|\n| `text/2026-11-17_arcom-presentation.md` | All 31 slides of the ARCOM deck: plans, elevations, suites and SF, variances, materials palette |\n| `text/2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2.md` | Lighting email thread, specs for all 4 Round 2 lanterns, comparison, open questions |\n\n## Folder layout (GitHub: svetlanawunnenberg/125worth)\n- `sources/<date>_<name>/`: original files as received\n- `pages/<name>/`: slide or page images\n- `text/`: searchable write-ups (mirrored to the claude.ai Project)\n"}, {"id": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "file": "text/2026-11-17_arcom-presentation.md", "title": "125 Worth Avenue: Town Council / ARCOM Presentation (50% Design Development)", "md": "# 125 Worth Avenue: Town Council / ARCOM Presentation (50% Design Development)\n\n- **Source file:** `sources/2026-11-17_arcom-presentation/125_Worth_Ave_TC_ARCOM_Presentation.pdf`\n- **Slide images:** `pages/arcom-presentation/slide-01.jpg` to `slide-31.jpg`\n- **Received:** November 17, 2026, from Michael Cabrera (Fairfax & Sammons), along with the architectural scope narrative\n- **Presentation date on cover:** December 9, 2026\n- **Prepared by:** Fairfax & Sammons Architects (New York / Palm Beach) for L3 Capital (ownership)\n- **Length:** 31 slides\n- **Keywords:** ARCOM, Town Council, Palm Beach, Worth Avenue, loggia, arcade, piers, storefronts, Mediterranean Revival, Mizner, variances, lighting, flooring, wall tile, ceiling, wall color, penthouse, pergola\n\n## Project summary (from the deck)\n\nThe deck proposes a renovation of an existing three-story building at 125 Worth Avenue. The main moves are:\n\n- Replacing the ground-floor columns with **piers**, so the arches end at a pier instead of a column.\n- Widening the Worth Avenue **loggia** from about **4'-0\" to about 10'-0\"**.\n- Removing the ground-floor glass **curtain wall** and putting in new **storefronts**.\n- Adding retail floor area **below the existing second-floor cantilever**, where there is parking today.\n- Replacing windows and doors on floors 2 and 3.\n- Upgrading the mechanical penthouse, with a **pergola**, a modified parapet and **45% open screening**.\n- Adding a **green space** at the northwest corner.\n\nThe ground floor becomes **six retail suites** plus a lobby. Finished floor elevation (F.F.E.) is **+12.64'** throughout.\n\n## Slide by slide\n\n**Slide 1: Cover.** L3 Capital logo. Rendered elevation of the full Worth Ave facade: white stucco, red tile roofs, three stories of arched openings, palms along the street. Title \"125 Worth Avenue, Palm Beach, December 9, 2026.\" Fairfax & Sammons laurel logo.\n\n**Slide 2: Location Plan.** Site plan of the block between Peruvian Ave (north) and Worth Ave (south). 125 Worth Ave (parcel 0380) is shaded. Neighbors: 151 Worth Ave (0130) and 175 Worth Ave to the west, 101 Worth Ave and Kirkland House to the east, condos along Peruvian (Peruvian Court Condo, Regency, Chalet by the Ocean, Mid-Ocean Condo, Peruvian Condo). Across Worth Ave: 150 Worth Ave (0010, the Esplanade) and Winthrop.\n\n**Slide 3: Existing Conditions.** Eight photos of the existing building today: a white three-story modern-Mediterranean block with arched balcony openings on floors 2 and 3, a dark glass curtain wall at grade, and open parking under the cantilevered upper floors on the north and side elevations.\n\n**Slide 4: Worth Ave Facade Alterations.** Inspiration photo: a Roman insula, the House of Diana, Ostia Antica, Rome (brick arcade with piers). Two 3D diagrams. Existing condition: arches end at a column, with the glass curtain wall beyond. Proposed condition (highlighted red): arches end at a pier, with storefront windows and associated infill. Narrative: replacing the columns with piers visually grounds and completes the facade, manages the building's load path better, and supports cantilevered elements such as the upper balconies.\n\n**Slide 5: Worth Ave Loggia Widening.** Existing and proposed floor plans, with enlarged partial plans. The existing loggia depth is about 4'-0\"; the proposed loggia is about 10'-0\" (yellow arrows). F.F.E. +12.64'.\n\n**Slide 6: Ground Floor Retail Expansion.** Infill diagram. Pink-hatched zones show added retail floor area below the existing second-floor cantilever, where the drive aisle and existing parking are today. The remaining parking is on the east side.\n\n**Slide 7: Massing Diagrams.** Two 3D massing views with the added ground-floor retail area in red, under the second-floor cantilever.\n\n**Slide 8: Proposed Green Space.** Ground floor plan with a green lawn area in the northwest corner (L-shaped, along the north property line) and a parking lot with 9 spaces (including accessible spaces) on the east.\n\n**Slide 9: Proposed Landscape Plan.** Color aerial site plan: palms along the Worth Ave sidewalk and the east and west edges, a hedged garden with a central feature in the northwest green space, a large specimen tree at the north, and cars in the east lot.\n\n**Slide 10: Proposed Ground Floor Plan.** Six suites along Worth Ave, west to east:\n| Suite | Area |\n|---|---|\n| Suite 1 | 3,599 SF |\n| Suite 2 | 3,900 SF |\n| Lobby (between 2 and 3) | |\n| Suite 3 | 1,740 SF |\n| Suite 4 | 2,383 SF |\n| Suite 5 | 1,552 SF |\n| Suite 6 | 713 SF |\n\nAlso shown: the stair/elevator core at the lobby, a rear lobby, trash/recycling, a shipping/receiving area, an electrical room, the parking lot with 9 numbered spaces, and a ramp at the northeast. F.F.E. +12.64' in each suite; the east grade is around +11.88'.\n\n**Slide 11: Demolition Elevations.** South, east, west and north elevations with demolition marked in red: removal of the ground-floor curtain wall, window and door replacement throughout floors 2 and 3, and removal of the ground-floor masonry walls at the east, west and north elevations.\n\n**Slide 12: Proposed Loggia & Typical Doors & Windows.** Elevations of the typical storefront types: a single door with sidelights, a three-panel window, and a wide multi-panel storefront with doors. Below them, four arched/framed storefront bays with a \"SIGNAGE\" band and a transom above each. Bottom: the full proposed south loggia elevation, a continuous arcade of arched bays with storefronts behind.\n\n**Slide 13: Existing & Proposed Wall Section.** Side-by-side building sections through the Worth Ave facade. Yellow arrows compare the narrower existing loggia with the deeper proposed one. The proposed section shows the new pier and arch, the loggia ceiling, and the storefront set back behind it.\n\n**Slide 14: Mechanical Penthouse Upgrades.** Existing and proposed roof and parapet sections. Proposed: a new barrel vault tile roof, a new GFRC cornice, a new cast stone cap, a modified parapet wall, a **proposed pergola**, **45% open area screening** around the mechanical equipment, a **proposed 3rd floor balcony**, and new windows and doors. The existing ornament and capital are to be removed, and the existing metal window replaced.\n\n**Slide 15: Proposed Penthouse Plan.** Roof plan. The existing mechanical penthouse remains (6,785 SF, unchanged). New cooling units sit around it, with a refrigerant chase, and screening at the loggia (45% open) on the east and west.\n\n**Slide 16: Existing & Proposed South Elevation (Worth Ave).** Existing: arches end at columns, with dark glazing at grade. Proposed: a continuous arcade on piers at grade, balconies with railings on floors 2 and 3, and a central arched loggia at the top floor under red tile roofs.\n\n**Slide 17: Existing & Proposed East Elevation.** The proposed elevation shows new arched openings at grade where the masonry is removed, new balconies, and roof modifications.\n\n**Slide 18: Existing & Proposed West Elevation.** The proposed elevation shows ground-floor infill under the cantilever and updated openings on the upper floors.\n\n**Slide 19: Existing & Proposed North Elevation.** Rear elevation facing the parking and green space, with column grid lines. The proposed elevation shows new ground-floor openings and doors, a loading area (a truck is drawn), and updated upper-floor windows. **This is the elevation related to the \"North interior loggia elevation\" mentioned in Michael's November 18 email.**\n\n**Slides 20 to 22: Existing & Proposed Renderings (marked \"In Development\").** Three pairs of street-level renderings, existing next to proposed. The proposed versions show the deeper arcade on piers, warmer cream stucco, wood-framed openings, and vines and landscaping at the corners. Slide 20 is the Worth Ave corner view; slide 21 is a lower corner view with a car; slide 22 is an elevated corner view.\n\n**Slide 23: Proposed Trucking & Logistics Plan.** Aerial map of the island with proposed truck ingress and egress routes (blue and red) to the site, plus a construction duration and schedule table.\n\n**Slide 24: Special Allowances & Variances.**\nSpecial allowances (per the Worth Avenue Design Guidelines):\n- To permit three stories in the C-WA zoning district.*\n- Lot coverage of 57.3% on the first floor and 64.3% on the second and third floors.*\n- Eliminating the maximum building length to allow 201.83' instead of 150'.*\n- Eliminating the 15,000 SF maximum gross floor area, allowing 9,168 SF over the existing 59,504 SF for a 68,672 SF building.*\n\nVariances:\n1. Parking reduced from 261 required spaces to 136 provided.\n2. Off-street loading spaces reduced from 3 required to 1 proposed space, backing a loading/staging area.\n3. Lot coverage of 64.3% on the third floor instead of the 30% maximum.\n4. Landscape open space of 5.9% instead of the 25% minimum (5.9% existing, 15.4% perceived).\n5. Exceeding the maximum overall height by 7.75'. The overall building is 47.75', above the 40' allowance.*\n\n\\*Existing non-conforming conditions.\n\n**Slide 25: Materials Palette, Lighting (Round 1 fixtures).**\n- **Lantern:** Hinkley **Palma** Large Hanging Lantern, SKU 26092BU, Burnished Bronze (also comes in Black), aluminum, clear glass, 12\" W x 21.5\" H. A simple tapered hexagonal lantern with a leaf crown.\n- **Sconce:** **Primo Lanterns BB-19E** (sold through Ferguson Home), a copper wall-mounted electric lantern with two candelabra lamps.\n- Elevation and reflected ceiling plan (RCP) of the loggia storefronts show lanterns hung at the bays and sconces flanking the doors.\n- These Round 1 fixtures are the ones Richard Sammons called \"too simplified\" (see the lighting thread of November 17 and 18).\n\n**Slide 26: Materials Palette, Flooring.**\n- Loggia flooring: **coquina or concrete stone**, as slab or a **3 to 4 ft chevron pattern**.\n- Steps: **cast stone treads and risers**, from **Haddonstone** (Acanthus cast stone products, \"Steps, T2 Treads\" detail). Color samples: Ivory, Bathstone, Cotswold, Yorkstone.\n\n**Slide 27: Materials Palette, Wall Tile.**\n- Field tile: **Regts Delft Tiles**, a blue and white star/geometric pattern.\n- Mosaic: an iridescent patterned mosaic.\n- Base tile: a gray stone-look tile.\n- The elevation shows the tile wainscot zone at the base of the storefront walls (red dashed box).\n\n**Slide 28: Materials Palette, Ceiling Material.**\n- Two options: **wood** (exposed wood beams and boards with a hanging lantern) or **stucco** (smooth stucco soffit).\n- Elevation and RCP of the loggia.\n\n**Slide 29: Ceiling references (no title).** Left: an arcade with an exposed wood-beam ceiling, stone arches and a tall hanging iron lantern. **This is the key reference for the loggia beam detail and lantern.** Right: a stucco soffit on a covered porch with a wood post.\n\n**Slide 30: Materials Palette, Wall Color.**\n- **Farrow & Ball Wimborne White No. 239.**\n- A Farrow & Ball color card is shown, along with a storefront elevation in the cream wall color with lanterns and sconces.\n\n**Slide 31: Proposed Loggia Massing Renderings.** Two interior loggia views: looking down the length of the loggia past \"SIGNAGE\" storefronts, with recessed downlights in a smooth ceiling and stone paving; and looking out through the arches toward the street, with a hedge between the piers. These are early massing renders, before lighting fixtures and beams were added.\n"}, {"id": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "file": "text/2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2.md", "title": "125 Worth Avenue: Loggia Lighting, Round 2 Lantern Options", "md": "# 125 Worth Avenue: Loggia Lighting, Round 2 Lantern Options\n\n- **Source folder:** `sources/2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2/`\n- **Received:** November 18, 2026, 8:30 AM. Michael Cabrera forwarded Raquel Swearingen's November 17 revisions.\n- **Keywords:** lanterns, pendants, sconces, step lights, photometrics, footcandles, property line, Mizner, Remains Lighting, Fine Art Lamps, Hinkley, Troy, Hudson Valley, Bexley Manor, Great Outdoors, Minka Lavery, beam detail, north interior loggia elevation\n\n## Action items (Michael Cabrera, Nov 18)\n\n- **Johnathan and Richard:** select one of the Round 2 lantern options and add it to the **North interior loggia elevation**, together with the **beam detail** Richard is developing.\n- The team needs an **enlarged section of the beam detail** to add to the drawings, and for **Svetlana to use as a reference for the rendering**.\n- The email went to Richard, Johnathan, William, Murat, Anne and Svetlana.\n\n## Email thread (oldest first)\n\n**Nov 17, 9:35 AM, Michael to Raquel** (cc Danele Spoden, Murat Saryyev). Michael asked for the loggia fixture options by 2:00 PM for a 2:30 PM materials review meeting with Ownership.\n\n**Nov 17, 12:10 PM, Raquel to Michael. Round 1 photometrics and fixture schedule.**\n- She used very low wattage lamps in the lanterns (**3W**) because the property line is so close. The town does not allow more than **0.5 footcandles over the property line**.\n- As a result the loggia is dim: about **2 footcandles**, where 5 to 10 is typical. She recommended submitting and installing as shown, and swapping in higher-wattage lamps later if tenants find it too dim.\n- She recommended keeping **the same size lanterns throughout**. Larger lanterns at the main entrance would likely have **4 lamps instead of 3** and spill too much light over the property line.\n- She moved the **F2 step lights** to the interior side of the columns to reduce spill over the property line.\n\n**Nov 17, 2:55 PM, Michael to Raquel. Feedback from Richard and Ownership.**\n- **Step lights:** asked for a recessed option. With the new piers, step lights can be flush or recessed instead of wall-mounted.\n- **Hanging pendants:** Richard found the selections too simplified and lacking the detail found throughout Worth Ave. To go over better with the architectural commissions, he wants options that are more true to the style and more ornate, not diluted. Suggested references: **Remains Lighting**, the Bonnet House article on Mizner lanterns and their replicas (bonnethouse.org/from-the-collections-vault-mizner-lanterns-and-their-replicas/), and the Remains blog on Addison Mizner artisanship (remains.com/blogs/blog/blog-addison-mizner-award-winning-artisanship).\n\n**Nov 17, 4:47 PM, Raquel to Michael** (cc Whitney Robinette of L3 Capital, Danele Spoden, Murat Saryyev, Richard Sammons). Sent revisions: added recessed step light options, and redid the lantern and sconce selections based on the feedback. She wanted time to refine before **Friday** (November 20, 2026).\n\n**Nov 18, 8:30 AM, Michael to the team.** Forwarded the Round 2 lanterns with the action items above. The email had 7 attachments; the 4 lantern spec sheets are archived here.\n\n## The four Round 2 lanterns\n\n### 1. Fine Art Lamps, Style 837082\n![Fine Art Lamps 837082 spec sheet](../pages/lanterns/FineArtLamps_837082.jpg)\n\n- Customer reference drawing dated 5/09/14. Fine Art Lamps (est. 1940), (305) 821-3850.\n- **34\" H x 15\" W**, 13\" across the flats (hexagonal).\n- Canopy 7\".\n- Overall hanging height: min 40\", max 109\".\n- Chain hung, with a crown cap and a tapered bottom on scroll brackets.\n- **Not on the sheet:** finish, material, lamp count, wattage, wet/damp rating. Confirm it's still in production (drawing is from 2014).\n\n### 2. Bexley Manor 9324-585 (The Great Outdoors / Minka Lavery)\n![Bexley Manor 9324-585 spec sheet](../pages/lanterns/BexleyManor_9324-585.jpg)\n\n- 4-light outdoor chain-hung pendant. Finish: black with gold highlights.\n- **31.75\" H x 11\" W x 11\" L**. Weight 15.54 lb.\n- Canopy 5\" diameter.\n- Chain 72\", wire 120\". Overall height: min 35.75\", max 106.25\".\n- **4 x 40W candelabra (E12) B10.5 incandescent**, dimmable.\n- Clear beveled bound glass, 4 panels (replacement SKU G9322-1).\n- Damp rated, ETL listed (certificate 4009012). UPC 747396097848.\n\n### 3. Troy Lighting Campanile F5009-FRN (Hudson Valley Lighting Group)\n![Troy Campanile F5009-FRN spec sheet](../pages/lanterns/Troy_Campanile_F5009-FRN.jpg)\n\n- Clear seeded glass globe caged in scrolling French Iron metalwork with a circle-motif band and acanthus-like leaves. Finish: French Iron.\n- **29.25\" H x 16.75\" diameter**. Weight 23.1 lb.\n- Canopy 6\", round.\n- 54\" chain, 120\" wire. Overall height: min 31\", max 97.25\". Sloped ceiling compatible.\n- **4 x E12 candelabra B11, 60W max.**\n- Replacement glass GLS5009-CD. cUL Wet rated. Made in Vietnam.\n- **Coastal suitable finish (EPM): No.**\n\n### 4. Hinkley Windsor 1892RK, Large Hanging Lantern (Estate Series)\n![Hinkley Windsor 1892RK spec sheet](../pages/lanterns/Hinkley_Windsor_1892RK.jpg)\n\n- Cast aluminum. Finish: River Rock (also Black). Clear water glass.\n- **28.5\" H x 12.5\" W**. Weight 16.0 lb.\n- Canopy 5.5\" diameter. Lead wire 72\". Fits sloped ceilings up to 90 degrees.\n- Socketed; **4 to 5W candelabra LED (40W equivalent)**, 120V, dimmable with a dimmable lamp.\n- Damp rated. 2-year warranty on outdoor finishes.\n\n## Comparison and recommendation (Svetlana and Claude, Nov 18)\n\n| Option | Size | Style | Concerns |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fine Art Lamps 837082 | 34\"H x 15\"W | Hexagonal cage, crown cap, scroll-bracket base. The closest match to the Mizner / Remains / Via Mizner references | Sheet has no finish, lamp count or rating; check availability |\n| Hinkley Windsor 1892RK | 28.5\"H x 12.5\"W | Traditional, but reads as a mass-produced cast piece | Best for salt air (aluminum, LED); good backup |\n| Troy Campanile F5009-FRN | 29.25\"H x 16.75\"W | Ornate, but the French lace globe reads French, not Spanish/Mediterranean | Not coastal-rated; 4 lamps |\n| Bexley Manor 9324-585 | 31.75\"H x 11\"W | Round, diamond-pattern glass; least detailed | 4 lamps, damp only |\n\n**Leading pick:** Fine Art Lamps 837082. It also resembles the hanging lantern in the slide 29 reference (wood-beam arcade).\n\n**Open questions for Raquel / Fine Art Lamps:**\n1. Finish options (aged iron or bronze?) and material.\n2. Lamp count and whether it works with the 3W lamping and the 0.5 fc property-line limit. 4-lamp fixtures were flagged as a spill risk.\n3. Wet/damp and coastal rating.\n4. Availability and lead time (drawing dated 2014).\n5. Hang height and chain length to clear the bottom of the new loggia beams.\n\n## Rendering reference: Via Mizner\n\n![Via Mizner lantern reference](../pages/lanterns/reference_via_mizner.jpg)\n\n`sources/2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2/reference_via_mizner_lantern.png` is a photo of a Palm Beach via: white stucco walls, a red tile roof, a stucco arch spanning the passage, cream scalloped awnings, red shutters, potted coleus and tropical planting, stone paving, and a **black iron lantern on a scrolled wall bracket**. Use it for mood and lantern character.\n\n## Related\n\n- ARCOM presentation, slide 25 (Round 1: Hinkley Palma + Primo BB-19E), slide 28 (ceiling options), slide 29 (wood-beam arcade reference), slide 19 (north elevation).\n"}], "chunks": [{"doc": "00_INDEX", "section": "125 Worth Avenue: Project Base, Master Index", "title": "125 Worth Avenue: Project Base, Master Index", "text": "The searchable knowledge base for the 125 Worth Avenue renovation in Palm Beach, FL. Start here.", "images": []}, {"doc": "00_INDEX", "section": "Project at a glance", "title": "Project at a glance", "text": "- **Address:** 125 Worth Avenue, Palm Beach, FL (parcel 0380), on the north side of Worth Ave between 151 Worth Ave (west) and 101 Worth Ave / Kirkland House (east). Across the street: 150 Worth Ave (the Esplanade).\n- **Owner:** L3 Capital (contact: Whitney Robinette).\n- **Architect:** Fairfax & Sammons Architects (New York and Palm Beach).\n- **Lighting consultant:** CX Connect (Raquel Swearingen).\n- **Status (Nov 2026):** 50% Design Development. ARCOM / Town Council presentation dated **December 9, 2026**.\n- **Scope:** piers replacing columns on Worth Ave; loggia widened from about 4' to about 10'; new storefronts; ground-floor retail infill under the 2nd-floor cantilever; 6 retail suites; window and door replacement on floors 2 and 3; penthouse pergola and mechanical screening; northwest green space.\n- **Building:** 3 stories, 68,672 SF proposed (59,504 SF existing), 47.75' overall height, F.F.E. +12.64'.", "images": []}, {"doc": "00_INDEX", "section": "People", "title": "People", "text": "| Name | Role | Contact |\n|---|---|---|\n| Michael Cabrera, Assoc. AIA | Associate, Fairfax & Sammons; runs the project day to day | mcabrera@fairfaxandsammons.com, 212-255-0704 x212 |\n| Richard Sammons | Principal, Fairfax & Sammons; developing the loggia beam detail; style direction | rsammons@fairfaxandsammons.com |\n| Anne Fairfax | Principal, Fairfax & Sammons | |\n| Murat Saryyev | Fairfax & Sammons | msaryyev@fairfaxandsammons.com |\n| Johnathan | Fairfax & Sammons; adding the lantern to the north interior loggia elevation | |\n| William | Fairfax & Sammons | |\n| Svetlana Wunnenberg | Visuals and marketing; renderings | |\n| Raquel Swearingen | Lighting consultant, CX Connect | Raquel.Swearingen@cxconnect.com, O 847-499-8352 |\n| Whitney Robinette | Ownership, L3 Capital | whitney.robinette@l3capital.com |\n| Danele Spoden | RLE Partners | dspoden@rlepartners.com |\n\nFairfax & Sammons offices: 2 Sniffen Court, New York, NY 10016, and 189 Bradley Place, Palm Beach, FL 33480 (new Palm Beach address).", "images": []}, {"doc": "00_INDEX", "section": "Timeline", "title": "Timeline", "text": "| Date | Event | Document |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026-11-17 | 50% DD presentation and scope narrative shared | `2026-11-17_arcom-presentation.md` |\n| 2026-11-17 | Round 1 lighting (Hinkley Palma, Primo BB-19E); materials review with Ownership at 2:30 PM | slide 25; `2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2.md` |\n| 2026-11-17 | Richard's feedback: more ornate, true Mizner style; recessed step lights | `2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2.md` |\n| 2026-11-18 | Round 2 lanterns forwarded; pick one for the north interior loggia elevation; beam detail section needed for rendering | `2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2.md` |\n| 2026-11-20 | Friday target for refinements (Raquel) | |\n| 2026-12-09 | ARCOM / Town Council presentation date | |", "images": []}, {"doc": "00_INDEX", "section": "Key decisions and constraints", "title": "Key decisions and constraints", "text": "- **Light spill:** no more than 0.5 footcandles over the property line. The lanterns use 3W lamps; the loggia sits at about 2 fc (5 to 10 is typical). Use the same size lantern throughout; avoid 4-lamp fixtures.\n- **Lantern style:** Richard wants ornate, authentic Mizner-style lanterns (Remains Lighting, Bonnet House references). Round 1 was rejected as too simplified.\n- **Step lights:** recessed or flush in the new piers.\n- **Materials:** coquina or concrete stone loggia floor (slab or 3 to 4 ft chevron); Haddonstone cast stone treads; Regts Delft field tile; wood-beam or stucco loggia ceiling; Farrow & Ball Wimborne White No. 239 walls.", "images": []}, {"doc": "00_INDEX", "section": "Documents in the base", "title": "Documents in the base", "text": "| File | What it covers |\n|---|---|\n| `text/2026-11-17_arcom-presentation.md` | All 31 slides of the ARCOM deck: plans, elevations, suites and SF, variances, materials palette |\n| `text/2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2.md` | Lighting email thread, specs for all 4 Round 2 lanterns, comparison, open questions |", "images": []}, {"doc": "00_INDEX", "section": "Folder layout (GitHub: svetlanawunnenberg/125worth)", "title": "Folder layout (GitHub: svetlanawunnenberg/125worth)", "text": "- `sources/<date>_<name>/`: original files as received\n- `pages/<name>/`: slide or page images\n- `text/`: searchable write-ups (mirrored to the claude.ai Project)", "images": []}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "125 Worth Avenue: Town Council / ARCOM Presentation (50% Design Development)", "title": "125 Worth Avenue: Town Council / ARCOM Presentation (50% Design Development)", "text": "- **Source file:** `sources/2026-11-17_arcom-presentation/125_Worth_Ave_TC_ARCOM_Presentation.pdf`\n- **Slide images:** `pages/arcom-presentation/slide-01.jpg` to `slide-31.jpg`\n- **Received:** November 17, 2026, from Michael Cabrera (Fairfax & Sammons), along with the architectural scope narrative\n- **Presentation date on cover:** December 9, 2026\n- **Prepared by:** Fairfax & Sammons Architects (New York / Palm Beach) for L3 Capital (ownership)\n- **Length:** 31 slides\n- **Keywords:** ARCOM, Town Council, Palm Beach, Worth Avenue, loggia, arcade, piers, storefronts, Mediterranean Revival, Mizner, variances, lighting, flooring, wall tile, ceiling, wall color, penthouse, pergola", "images": []}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Project summary (from the deck)", "title": "Project summary (from the deck)", "text": "The deck proposes a renovation of an existing three-story building at 125 Worth Avenue. The main moves are:\n\n- Replacing the ground-floor columns with **piers**, so the arches end at a pier instead of a column.\n- Widening the Worth Avenue **loggia** from about **4'-0\" to about 10'-0\"**.\n- Removing the ground-floor glass **curtain wall** and putting in new **storefronts**.\n- Adding retail floor area **below the existing second-floor cantilever**, where there is parking today.\n- Replacing windows and doors on floors 2 and 3.\n- Upgrading the mechanical penthouse, with a **pergola**, a modified parapet and **45% open screening**.\n- Adding a **green space** at the northwest corner.\n\nThe ground floor becomes **six retail suites** plus a lobby. Finished floor elevation (F.F.E.) is **+12.64'** throughout.", "images": []}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 1: Cover", "text": "**Slide 1: Cover.** L3 Capital logo. Rendered elevation of the full Worth Ave facade: white stucco, red tile roofs, three stories of arched openings, palms along the street. Title \"125 Worth Avenue, Palm Beach, December 9, 2026.\" Fairfax & Sammons laurel logo.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-01.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 2: Location Plan", "text": "**Slide 2: Location Plan.** Site plan of the block between Peruvian Ave (north) and Worth Ave (south). 125 Worth Ave (parcel 0380) is shaded. Neighbors: 151 Worth Ave (0130) and 175 Worth Ave to the west, 101 Worth Ave and Kirkland House to the east, condos along Peruvian (Peruvian Court Condo, Regency, Chalet by the Ocean, Mid-Ocean Condo, Peruvian Condo). Across Worth Ave: 150 Worth Ave (0010, the Esplanade) and Winthrop.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-02.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 3: Existing Conditions", "text": "**Slide 3: Existing Conditions.** Eight photos of the existing building today: a white three-story modern-Mediterranean block with arched balcony openings on floors 2 and 3, a dark glass curtain wall at grade, and open parking under the cantilevered upper floors on the north and side elevations.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-03.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 4: Worth Ave Facade Alterations", "text": "**Slide 4: Worth Ave Facade Alterations.** Inspiration photo: a Roman insula, the House of Diana, Ostia Antica, Rome (brick arcade with piers). Two 3D diagrams. Existing condition: arches end at a column, with the glass curtain wall beyond. Proposed condition (highlighted red): arches end at a pier, with storefront windows and associated infill. Narrative: replacing the columns with piers visually grounds and completes the facade, manages the building's load path better, and supports cantilevered elements such as the upper balconies.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-04.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 5: Worth Ave Loggia Widening", "text": "**Slide 5: Worth Ave Loggia Widening.** Existing and proposed floor plans, with enlarged partial plans. The existing loggia depth is about 4'-0\"; the proposed loggia is about 10'-0\" (yellow arrows). F.F.E. +12.64'.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-05.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 6: Ground Floor Retail Expansion", "text": "**Slide 6: Ground Floor Retail Expansion.** Infill diagram. Pink-hatched zones show added retail floor area below the existing second-floor cantilever, where the drive aisle and existing parking are today. The remaining parking is on the east side.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-06.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 7: Massing Diagrams", "text": "**Slide 7: Massing Diagrams.** Two 3D massing views with the added ground-floor retail area in red, under the second-floor cantilever.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-07.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 8: Proposed Green Space", "text": "**Slide 8: Proposed Green Space.** Ground floor plan with a green lawn area in the northwest corner (L-shaped, along the north property line) and a parking lot with 9 spaces (including accessible spaces) on the east.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-08.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 9: Proposed Landscape Plan", "text": "**Slide 9: Proposed Landscape Plan.** Color aerial site plan: palms along the Worth Ave sidewalk and the east and west edges, a hedged garden with a central feature in the northwest green space, a large specimen tree at the north, and cars in the east lot.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-09.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 10: Proposed Ground Floor Plan", "text": "**Slide 10: Proposed Ground Floor Plan.** Six suites along Worth Ave, west to east:\n| Suite | Area |\n|---|---|\n| Suite 1 | 3,599 SF |\n| Suite 2 | 3,900 SF |\n| Lobby (between 2 and 3) | |\n| Suite 3 | 1,740 SF |\n| Suite 4 | 2,383 SF |\n| Suite 5 | 1,552 SF |\n| Suite 6 | 713 SF |\n\nAlso shown: the stair/elevator core at the lobby, a rear lobby, trash/recycling, a shipping/receiving area, an electrical room, the parking lot with 9 numbered spaces, and a ramp at the northeast. F.F.E. +12.64' in each suite; the east grade is around +11.88'.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-10.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 11: Demolition Elevations", "text": "**Slide 11: Demolition Elevations.** South, east, west and north elevations with demolition marked in red: removal of the ground-floor curtain wall, window and door replacement throughout floors 2 and 3, and removal of the ground-floor masonry walls at the east, west and north elevations.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-11.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 12: Proposed Loggia & Typical Doors & Windows", "text": "**Slide 12: Proposed Loggia & Typical Doors & Windows.** Elevations of the typical storefront types: a single door with sidelights, a three-panel window, and a wide multi-panel storefront with doors. Below them, four arched/framed storefront bays with a \"SIGNAGE\" band and a transom above each. Bottom: the full proposed south loggia elevation, a continuous arcade of arched bays with storefronts behind.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-12.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 13: Existing & Proposed Wall Section", "text": "**Slide 13: Existing & Proposed Wall Section.** Side-by-side building sections through the Worth Ave facade. Yellow arrows compare the narrower existing loggia with the deeper proposed one. The proposed section shows the new pier and arch, the loggia ceiling, and the storefront set back behind it.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-13.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 14: Mechanical Penthouse Upgrades", "text": "**Slide 14: Mechanical Penthouse Upgrades.** Existing and proposed roof and parapet sections. Proposed: a new barrel vault tile roof, a new GFRC cornice, a new cast stone cap, a modified parapet wall, a **proposed pergola**, **45% open area screening** around the mechanical equipment, a **proposed 3rd floor balcony**, and new windows and doors. The existing ornament and capital are to be removed, and the existing metal window replaced.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-14.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 15: Proposed Penthouse Plan", "text": "**Slide 15: Proposed Penthouse Plan.** Roof plan. The existing mechanical penthouse remains (6,785 SF, unchanged). New cooling units sit around it, with a refrigerant chase, and screening at the loggia (45% open) on the east and west.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-15.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 16: Existing & Proposed South Elevation (Worth Ave)", "text": "**Slide 16: Existing & Proposed South Elevation (Worth Ave).** Existing: arches end at columns, with dark glazing at grade. Proposed: a continuous arcade on piers at grade, balconies with railings on floors 2 and 3, and a central arched loggia at the top floor under red tile roofs.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-16.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 17: Existing & Proposed East Elevation", "text": "**Slide 17: Existing & Proposed East Elevation.** The proposed elevation shows new arched openings at grade where the masonry is removed, new balconies, and roof modifications.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-17.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 18: Existing & Proposed West Elevation", "text": "**Slide 18: Existing & Proposed West Elevation.** The proposed elevation shows ground-floor infill under the cantilever and updated openings on the upper floors.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-18.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 19: Existing & Proposed North Elevation", "text": "**Slide 19: Existing & Proposed North Elevation.** Rear elevation facing the parking and green space, with column grid lines. The proposed elevation shows new ground-floor openings and doors, a loading area (a truck is drawn), and updated upper-floor windows. **This is the elevation related to the \"North interior loggia elevation\" mentioned in Michael's November 18 email.**", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-19.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slides 20 to 22: Existing & Proposed Renderings (marked \"In Development\")", "text": "**Slides 20 to 22: Existing & Proposed Renderings (marked \"In Development\").** Three pairs of street-level renderings, existing next to proposed. The proposed versions show the deeper arcade on piers, warmer cream stucco, wood-framed openings, and vines and landscaping at the corners. Slide 20 is the Worth Ave corner view; slide 21 is a lower corner view with a car; slide 22 is an elevated corner view.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-20.jpg", "pages/arcom-presentation/slide-21.jpg", "pages/arcom-presentation/slide-22.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 23: Proposed Trucking & Logistics Plan", "text": "**Slide 23: Proposed Trucking & Logistics Plan.** Aerial map of the island with proposed truck ingress and egress routes (blue and red) to the site, plus a construction duration and schedule table.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-23.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 24: Special Allowances & Variances", "text": "**Slide 24: Special Allowances & Variances.**\nSpecial allowances (per the Worth Avenue Design Guidelines):\n- To permit three stories in the C-WA zoning district.*\n- Lot coverage of 57.3% on the first floor and 64.3% on the second and third floors.*\n- Eliminating the maximum building length to allow 201.83' instead of 150'.*\n- Eliminating the 15,000 SF maximum gross floor area, allowing 9,168 SF over the existing 59,504 SF for a 68,672 SF building.*\n\nVariances:\n1. Parking reduced from 261 required spaces to 136 provided.\n2. Off-street loading spaces reduced from 3 required to 1 proposed space, backing a loading/staging area.\n3. Lot coverage of 64.3% on the third floor instead of the 30% maximum.\n4. Landscape open space of 5.9% instead of the 25% minimum (5.9% existing, 15.4% perceived).\n5. Exceeding the maximum overall height by 7.75'. The overall building is 47.75', above the 40' allowance.*\n\n\\*Existing non-conforming conditions.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-24.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 25: Materials Palette, Lighting (Round 1 fixtures)", "text": "**Slide 25: Materials Palette, Lighting (Round 1 fixtures).**\n- **Lantern:** Hinkley **Palma** Large Hanging Lantern, SKU 26092BU, Burnished Bronze (also comes in Black), aluminum, clear glass, 12\" W x 21.5\" H. A simple tapered hexagonal lantern with a leaf crown.\n- **Sconce:** **Primo Lanterns BB-19E** (sold through Ferguson Home), a copper wall-mounted electric lantern with two candelabra lamps.\n- Elevation and reflected ceiling plan (RCP) of the loggia storefronts show lanterns hung at the bays and sconces flanking the doors.\n- These Round 1 fixtures are the ones Richard Sammons called \"too simplified\" (see the lighting thread of November 17 and 18).", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-25.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 26: Materials Palette, Flooring", "text": "**Slide 26: Materials Palette, Flooring.**\n- Loggia flooring: **coquina or concrete stone**, as slab or a **3 to 4 ft chevron pattern**.\n- Steps: **cast stone treads and risers**, from **Haddonstone** (Acanthus cast stone products, \"Steps, T2 Treads\" detail). Color samples: Ivory, Bathstone, Cotswold, Yorkstone.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-26.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 27: Materials Palette, Wall Tile", "text": "**Slide 27: Materials Palette, Wall Tile.**\n- Field tile: **Regts Delft Tiles**, a blue and white star/geometric pattern.\n- Mosaic: an iridescent patterned mosaic.\n- Base tile: a gray stone-look tile.\n- The elevation shows the tile wainscot zone at the base of the storefront walls (red dashed box).", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-27.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 28: Materials Palette, Ceiling Material", "text": "**Slide 28: Materials Palette, Ceiling Material.**\n- Two options: **wood** (exposed wood beams and boards with a hanging lantern) or **stucco** (smooth stucco soffit).\n- Elevation and RCP of the loggia.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-28.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 29: Ceiling references (no title)", "text": "**Slide 29: Ceiling references (no title).** Left: an arcade with an exposed wood-beam ceiling, stone arches and a tall hanging iron lantern. **This is the key reference for the loggia beam detail and lantern.** Right: a stucco soffit on a covered porch with a wood post.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-29.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 30: Materials Palette, Wall Color", "text": "**Slide 30: Materials Palette, Wall Color.**\n- **Farrow & Ball Wimborne White No. 239.**\n- A Farrow & Ball color card is shown, along with a storefront elevation in the cream wall color with lanterns and sconces.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-30.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-17_arcom-presentation", "section": "Slide by slide", "title": "Slide 31: Proposed Loggia Massing Renderings", "text": "**Slide 31: Proposed Loggia Massing Renderings.** Two interior loggia views: looking down the length of the loggia past \"SIGNAGE\" storefronts, with recessed downlights in a smooth ceiling and stone paving; and looking out through the arches toward the street, with a hedge between the piers. These are early massing renders, before lighting fixtures and beams were added.", "images": ["pages/arcom-presentation/slide-31.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "125 Worth Avenue: Loggia Lighting, Round 2 Lantern Options", "title": "125 Worth Avenue: Loggia Lighting, Round 2 Lantern Options", "text": "- **Source folder:** `sources/2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2/`\n- **Received:** November 18, 2026, 8:30 AM. Michael Cabrera forwarded Raquel Swearingen's November 17 revisions.\n- **Keywords:** lanterns, pendants, sconces, step lights, photometrics, footcandles, property line, Mizner, Remains Lighting, Fine Art Lamps, Hinkley, Troy, Hudson Valley, Bexley Manor, Great Outdoors, Minka Lavery, beam detail, north interior loggia elevation", "images": []}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "Action items (Michael Cabrera, Nov 18)", "title": "Action items (Michael Cabrera, Nov 18)", "text": "- **Johnathan and Richard:** select one of the Round 2 lantern options and add it to the **North interior loggia elevation**, together with the **beam detail** Richard is developing.\n- The team needs an **enlarged section of the beam detail** to add to the drawings, and for **Svetlana to use as a reference for the rendering**.\n- The email went to Richard, Johnathan, William, Murat, Anne and Svetlana.", "images": []}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "Email thread (oldest first)", "title": "Nov 17, 9:35 AM, Michael to Raquel", "text": "**Nov 17, 9:35 AM, Michael to Raquel** (cc Danele Spoden, Murat Saryyev). Michael asked for the loggia fixture options by 2:00 PM for a 2:30 PM materials review meeting with Ownership.", "images": []}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "Email thread (oldest first)", "title": "Nov 17, 12:10 PM, Raquel to Michael. Round 1 photometrics and fixture schedule", "text": "**Nov 17, 12:10 PM, Raquel to Michael. Round 1 photometrics and fixture schedule.**\n- She used very low wattage lamps in the lanterns (**3W**) because the property line is so close. The town does not allow more than **0.5 footcandles over the property line**.\n- As a result the loggia is dim: about **2 footcandles**, where 5 to 10 is typical. She recommended submitting and installing as shown, and swapping in higher-wattage lamps later if tenants find it too dim.\n- She recommended keeping **the same size lanterns throughout**. Larger lanterns at the main entrance would likely have **4 lamps instead of 3** and spill too much light over the property line.\n- She moved the **F2 step lights** to the interior side of the columns to reduce spill over the property line.", "images": []}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "Email thread (oldest first)", "title": "Nov 17, 2:55 PM, Michael to Raquel. Feedback from Richard and Ownership", "text": "**Nov 17, 2:55 PM, Michael to Raquel. Feedback from Richard and Ownership.**\n- **Step lights:** asked for a recessed option. With the new piers, step lights can be flush or recessed instead of wall-mounted.\n- **Hanging pendants:** Richard found the selections too simplified and lacking the detail found throughout Worth Ave. To go over better with the architectural commissions, he wants options that are more true to the style and more ornate, not diluted. Suggested references: **Remains Lighting**, the Bonnet House article on Mizner lanterns and their replicas (bonnethouse.org/from-the-collections-vault-mizner-lanterns-and-their-replicas/), and the Remains blog on Addison Mizner artisanship (remains.com/blogs/blog/blog-addison-mizner-award-winning-artisanship).", "images": []}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "Email thread (oldest first)", "title": "Nov 17, 4:47 PM, Raquel to Michael", "text": "**Nov 17, 4:47 PM, Raquel to Michael** (cc Whitney Robinette of L3 Capital, Danele Spoden, Murat Saryyev, Richard Sammons). Sent revisions: added recessed step light options, and redid the lantern and sconce selections based on the feedback. She wanted time to refine before **Friday** (November 20, 2026).", "images": []}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "Email thread (oldest first)", "title": "Nov 18, 8:30 AM, Michael to the team", "text": "**Nov 18, 8:30 AM, Michael to the team.** Forwarded the Round 2 lanterns with the action items above. The email had 7 attachments; the 4 lantern spec sheets are archived here.", "images": []}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "1. Fine Art Lamps, Style 837082", "title": "1. Fine Art Lamps, Style 837082", "text": "![Fine Art Lamps 837082 spec sheet](../pages/lanterns/FineArtLamps_837082.jpg)\n\n- Customer reference drawing dated 5/09/14. Fine Art Lamps (est. 1940), (305) 821-3850.\n- **34\" H x 15\" W**, 13\" across the flats (hexagonal).\n- Canopy 7\".\n- Overall hanging height: min 40\", max 109\".\n- Chain hung, with a crown cap and a tapered bottom on scroll brackets.\n- **Not on the sheet:** finish, material, lamp count, wattage, wet/damp rating. Confirm it's still in production (drawing is from 2014).", "images": ["pages/lanterns/FineArtLamps_837082.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "2. Bexley Manor 9324-585 (The Great Outdoors / Minka Lavery)", "title": "2. Bexley Manor 9324-585 (The Great Outdoors / Minka Lavery)", "text": "![Bexley Manor 9324-585 spec sheet](../pages/lanterns/BexleyManor_9324-585.jpg)\n\n- 4-light outdoor chain-hung pendant. Finish: black with gold highlights.\n- **31.75\" H x 11\" W x 11\" L**. Weight 15.54 lb.\n- Canopy 5\" diameter.\n- Chain 72\", wire 120\". Overall height: min 35.75\", max 106.25\".\n- **4 x 40W candelabra (E12) B10.5 incandescent**, dimmable.\n- Clear beveled bound glass, 4 panels (replacement SKU G9322-1).\n- Damp rated, ETL listed (certificate 4009012). UPC 747396097848.", "images": ["pages/lanterns/BexleyManor_9324-585.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "3. Troy Lighting Campanile F5009-FRN (Hudson Valley Lighting Group)", "title": "3. Troy Lighting Campanile F5009-FRN (Hudson Valley Lighting Group)", "text": "![Troy Campanile F5009-FRN spec sheet](../pages/lanterns/Troy_Campanile_F5009-FRN.jpg)\n\n- Clear seeded glass globe caged in scrolling French Iron metalwork with a circle-motif band and acanthus-like leaves. Finish: French Iron.\n- **29.25\" H x 16.75\" diameter**. Weight 23.1 lb.\n- Canopy 6\", round.\n- 54\" chain, 120\" wire. Overall height: min 31\", max 97.25\". Sloped ceiling compatible.\n- **4 x E12 candelabra B11, 60W max.**\n- Replacement glass GLS5009-CD. cUL Wet rated. Made in Vietnam.\n- **Coastal suitable finish (EPM): No.**", "images": ["pages/lanterns/Troy_Campanile_F5009-FRN.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "4. Hinkley Windsor 1892RK, Large Hanging Lantern (Estate Series)", "title": "4. Hinkley Windsor 1892RK, Large Hanging Lantern (Estate Series)", "text": "![Hinkley Windsor 1892RK spec sheet](../pages/lanterns/Hinkley_Windsor_1892RK.jpg)\n\n- Cast aluminum. Finish: River Rock (also Black). Clear water glass.\n- **28.5\" H x 12.5\" W**. Weight 16.0 lb.\n- Canopy 5.5\" diameter. Lead wire 72\". Fits sloped ceilings up to 90 degrees.\n- Socketed; **4 to 5W candelabra LED (40W equivalent)**, 120V, dimmable with a dimmable lamp.\n- Damp rated. 2-year warranty on outdoor finishes.", "images": ["pages/lanterns/Hinkley_Windsor_1892RK.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "Comparison and recommendation (Svetlana and Claude, Nov 18)", "title": "Comparison and recommendation (Svetlana and Claude, Nov 18)", "text": "| Option | Size | Style | Concerns |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fine Art Lamps 837082 | 34\"H x 15\"W | Hexagonal cage, crown cap, scroll-bracket base. The closest match to the Mizner / Remains / Via Mizner references | Sheet has no finish, lamp count or rating; check availability |\n| Hinkley Windsor 1892RK | 28.5\"H x 12.5\"W | Traditional, but reads as a mass-produced cast piece | Best for salt air (aluminum, LED); good backup |\n| Troy Campanile F5009-FRN | 29.25\"H x 16.75\"W | Ornate, but the French lace globe reads French, not Spanish/Mediterranean | Not coastal-rated; 4 lamps |\n| Bexley Manor 9324-585 | 31.75\"H x 11\"W | Round, diamond-pattern glass; least detailed | 4 lamps, damp only |", "images": []}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "Comparison and recommendation (Svetlana and Claude, Nov 18)", "title": "Leading pick", "text": "**Leading pick:** Fine Art Lamps 837082. It also resembles the hanging lantern in the slide 29 reference (wood-beam arcade).", "images": []}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "Comparison and recommendation (Svetlana and Claude, Nov 18)", "title": "Open questions for Raquel / Fine Art Lamps", "text": "**Open questions for Raquel / Fine Art Lamps:**\n1. Finish options (aged iron or bronze?) and material.\n2. Lamp count and whether it works with the 3W lamping and the 0.5 fc property-line limit. 4-lamp fixtures were flagged as a spill risk.\n3. Wet/damp and coastal rating.\n4. Availability and lead time (drawing dated 2014).\n5. Hang height and chain length to clear the bottom of the new loggia beams.", "images": []}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "Rendering reference: Via Mizner", "title": "Rendering reference: Via Mizner", "text": "![Via Mizner lantern reference](../pages/lanterns/reference_via_mizner.jpg)\n\n`sources/2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2/reference_via_mizner_lantern.png` is a photo of a Palm Beach via: white stucco walls, a red tile roof, a stucco arch spanning the passage, cream scalloped awnings, red shutters, potted coleus and tropical planting, stone paving, and a **black iron lantern on a scrolled wall bracket**. Use it for mood and lantern character.", "images": ["pages/lanterns/reference_via_mizner.jpg"]}, {"doc": "2026-11-18_lantern-options-round-2", "section": "Related", "title": "Related", "text": "- ARCOM presentation, slide 25 (Round 1: Hinkley Palma + Primo BB-19E), slide 28 (ceiling options), slide 29 (wood-beam arcade reference), slide 19 (north elevation).", "images": []}], "slides": ["slide-01.jpg", "slide-02.jpg", "slide-03.jpg", "slide-04.jpg", "slide-05.jpg", "slide-06.jpg", "slide-07.jpg", "slide-08.jpg", "slide-09.jpg", "slide-10.jpg", "slide-11.jpg", "slide-12.jpg", "slide-13.jpg", "slide-14.jpg", "slide-15.jpg", "slide-16.jpg", "slide-17.jpg", "slide-18.jpg", "slide-19.jpg", "slide-20.jpg", "slide-21.jpg", "slide-22.jpg", "slide-23.jpg", "slide-24.jpg", "slide-25.jpg", "slide-26.jpg", "slide-27.jpg", "slide-28.jpg", "slide-29.jpg", "slide-30.jpg", "slide-31.jpg"]};
